>>> extract_links('<p><a href="/home">Home</a> and <a href="https://example.com">Example <b>site</b></a></p>')
[('Home', '/home'), ('Example site', 'https://example.com')]
```

Notes on the behavior:
- It uses Python's standard `html.parser`, so tag and attribute names are case-insensitive, and entities like `&amp;` and `&#39;` are decoded in both text and attribute values.
- Text from nested tags (such as `<b>`) is included, and the tags themselves are dropped.
- An `<a>` without an `href` gives `None` for the href. An `href` with no value (e.g. `<a href>`) gives `''`.
- Whitespace in the text is kept as it appears in the source. If you want it trimmed or collapsed, that's a one-line change.

