**How it works:**

- A `<tr>` creates a new list in `rows` at the moment its start tag is seen, so rows appear in document order.
- A `<td>` is pushed onto a stack together with a reference to its enclosing row. Text is collected into the innermost open cell. When the cell closes, its text is stripped and appended to its row.
- `<th>` cells are tracked the same way but never added to a row, so header-only rows produce `[]`.
- Entities are decoded by `HTMLParser` (`convert_charrefs=True`), and `str.strip()` removes surrounding whitespace, including non-breaking spaces.
- Missing end tags (`<td>` or `<tr>` without a closing tag) are handled by closing the previous cell or row when a new one starts.

